<prompt>
  <role>
    You are a route-baseline planner for a family EV road trip with a rooftop
    tent. Your job is to compute the real Google-grounded route skeleton only.
  </role>

  <task>
    Return exactly one document:
    1. baseline_route.json
  </task>

  <method>
    <item>Start from trip_requirements.yaml. Treat it as the durable source of truth for fixed dates, required anchors, routing preferences, and hard constraints.</item>
    <item>Use route_guide.md as route-shaping guidance and selective inspiration. It helps choose anchor regions, route variants, and meaningful corridor towns, but it is not a mandatory checklist.</item>
    <item>Use Google Maps grounding whenever available. Normalize anchors, compute viable leg order, and generate real route options with real Google Maps directions links.</item>
    <item>Plan only the baseline corridor. Do not choose exact campsites, exact restaurants, or full day-by-day activities.</item>
    <item>The output must be rich enough for step 2 to search for stays and activities around each leg and anchor.</item>
  </method>

  <rules>
    <item>Return only baseline_route.json.</item>
    <item>Build the route shape implied by trip_requirements.yaml. If the trip starts and ends in the same place, emit a loop. If not, emit a one_way or open_jaw route shape as appropriate.</item>
    <item>Respect fixed anchors and fixed timing requirements, especially Monte Gordo and Barcelona for this trip.</item>
    <item>If the requirements call for a different return route, encode that explicitly with outbound and return anchor groups. Otherwise those groups may be omitted.</item>
    <item>Prefer anchor sequences and leg lengths that make later campsite planning realistic for a family with a rooftop tent.</item>
    <item>Do not treat a leg as a full drive day by default. Step 1 should emit corridor legs with enough granularity for step 2 to combine them into actual drive days.</item>
    <item>Prefer shorter legs between meaningful anchors, typically around 50 to 100 km when that is practical. Use longer legs only when there is no worthwhile intermediate anchor or corridor split.</item>
    <item>Every anchor must be geolocatable. Include an anchor_query and grounded geolocation fields for each anchor whenever available.</item>
    <item>For every drive leg, include at least two route options: fast and scenic. Add no_highways when feasible.</item>
    <item>Every route option must include a valid Google Maps directions link when Google Maps grounding was used.</item>
    <item>Every route option must include concrete road names or road numbers, ordered steps, and enough route detail that step 2 can understand the corridor.</item>
    <item>Every leg must include corridor hints for later stay/activity discovery, such as intermediate towns, regions, or arrival areas worth searching in step 2.</item>
    <item>Do not invent Google Maps links, durations, or roads. If a route fact is unknown, set it to null and explain briefly in notes.</item>
  </rules>

  <validation_checks>
    <item>The route start and end align with trip_requirements.yaml.</item>
    <item>Monte Gordo appears as a fixed anchor when required by the trip requirements.</item>
    <item>Barcelona appears as a fixed anchor or explicit family-visit anchor when required by the trip requirements.</item>
    <item>Every leg has at least 2 route options.</item>
    <item>Every fast or scenic route option has maps_url when Google-grounded.</item>
    <item>No maps_url may be a placeholder token or fabricated dummy string.</item>
    <item>Every route option has major_roads and steps.</item>
    <item>Every leg has corridor_search_hints that step 2 can use.</item>
    <item>Every anchor has anchor_query and geolocation fields.</item>
    <item>No preferred_drive_day_split field is emitted; step 2 decides how legs combine into actual drive days.</item>
  </validation_checks>

  <document title="Trip Requirements" syntax="yaml">
    Family Trip Requirements:
  Traveler Information:
  - Luis, 40
  - Sibylle, 40
  - Len, 5
  - Ida, 1
  Vehicle Information:
    Model: ID.Buzz GTX Long wheel base
    Sleeping setup: Thule Approach Size Large Rooftop tent
    Height: 2.4 meters
    Real-world range: ~350-400 km in summer with load
  Family needs, ordered top-down by priority:
  - All overnight stays should be suitable campsites for sleeping in a van with a rooftop tent (except for overnight stays already written in "Confirmed Decisions" section)
  - Campsites must have pitches for a van or camper
  - Campsites must provide **hot showers** and **clean toilets**
  - Favor quality over pace, with fewer base changes and more meaningful stays.
  - Choose campsites in locations with worthwhile places to visit and family-friendly things to do nearby within walking distance or by bike.
  - Campsites must be safe for small children, clean, quiet after **22:00**, and free of major hygiene or traffic-safety concerns.
  - Plan campsite base stays for at least **2 nights**, and prefer longer stays when the location is strong enough to justify them.
  - Favor fresh, local, seasonal food throughout the trip, especially seafood, strong produce access, artisan bakeries, local coffee roasters, micro local breweries and authentic regional restaurants.
  Campsite locations wishlist, ordered top-down by priority:
  - swimming pool,
  - playground or safe play space
  - soft-surface pitches
  - shaded areas with trees
  - and a family atmosphere with other young children around
  - bike rental on-site or nearby
  - charging infrastructure for EVs
  - nearby supermarket, grocery store or local food market
  - nearby bakery or market with fresh bread and baguette (specially in france)
  - nearby beach, park or town where we can walk and plan easy going activities during the day
  - kids club with entertainment activities for children
  - yoga classes for adults
  - Prefer private farm with animals, vineyard, or agritourism stays when they function as true campsite alternatives
  - nearby surfable beach break
  - nearby snorkeling or other water related activities
  - nearby trail access
  Itinerary planning constraints:
    General:
    - Every day must specify the exact sleep location
    - Every day must state whether its a drive day (we drive to a new sleep location) or a base day (we sleep in the same location)
    Every drive day must include:
    - Multiple lunch stop options
    - Multiple family rest options
    - Multiple EV charging options (ideally combining lunch and rest)
    - Multiple interesting waypoints along the route to stop, visit, do and see
    - Find suggestions whenever possible where we can buy fresh food ingredients from local food producer or markets along the route
    - fast route option
    - scenic route option
    Every base day must include:
    - 'interesting things to visit, do and see so we can discover the area like for example: going to the beach, doing a small kids-friendly trail, going to visit the local town, etc'
    - grocery, local market, local bakery or local restaurant suggestions reachable on foot or by bike without moving the vehicle
  Driving preferences:
    General:
    - Offer both a **fast route option** and a **scenic route option** for every drive day, plus a **no-highway option** when feasible.
    - Favor scenic and low-stress routing when it does not create a meaningful downside.
    - Avoid stressful city-center driving and prefer park-and-walk approaches for city visits.
    - Prefer to keep most drive days at or below **400 km**.
    Allow a longer drive day only when:
    - there is no high-value stop in between,
    - and the extra distance buys at least one additional stationary night at a better base.
  What to Avoid:
  - Fast food, chains, and heavily processed food.
  - Single-night campsite stops without a strong reason.
  - Campsites with poor hygiene, poor safety reviews, or a loud late-night atmosphere.
  Confirmed Decisions:
  - Return to Zurich by **2026-07-10**.
  - Departure is ideally **2026-06-04**, with **2026-06-05** still acceptable if needed.
  - '**Monte Gordo fixed anchor:** Hotel Dunas Mar, **2026-06-22 to 2026-06-29**, pre-booked and outside the camping itinerary.'
  - '**Barcelona fixed anchor** is a family visit for **1 to 2 nights** and does not need campsite planning.'
  - Return routing should differ from the outbound route
  - '**Douro Valley** is intentionally excluded from this trip.'
  </document>

  <document title="Route Guide" syntax="markdown">
    # 🗺️ Mediterranean Loop — Trip Planning Guide

---

## 1. Route Backbone

### Route Options & Trade-offs

Two versions of the loop were mapped. Both share the same core arc; the only difference is whether you extend into southeast Spain via Cartagena.

**Option A — Main Mediterranean Arc (full loop)**

> Zurich → Marseille → Montpellier → Santa Cristina d'Aro → Barcelona → Valencia → **Cartagena** → Sevilla → Monte Gordo → Aljezur → Salamanca → Getaria → Hossegor → Biarritz → Clermont-Ferrand → Zurich

Google Maps total driving time: **~2 days 6 hours**

**Option B — Shorter Inland-Cut Version**

> Zurich → Marseille → Montpellier → Santa Cristina d'Aro → Barcelona → Valencia → Sevilla → Monte Gordo → Aljezur → Salamanca → Getaria → Hossegor → Biarritz → Clermont-Ferrand → Zurich

Google Maps total driving time: **~2 days 4 hours**

**Trade-off:** The Cartagena version only adds about 2 hours of total driving time. That makes it viable if you prioritise the southeast coast experience — the volcanic Cabo de Gata landscape and the raw Cala de San Pedro cove — over minimising drive time.

---

### Driving Legs — Distances & Times

| Leg | km | Drive time |
|---|---|---|
| Zurich → Marseille | 733 | 7h 12m |
| Marseille → Montpellier | 169 | 1h 55m |
| Montpellier → Costa Brava / Girona | 254 | 2h 52m |
| Costa Brava → Barcelona | 102 | 1h 25m |
| Barcelona → Valencia | 348 | 3h 44m |
| Valencia → Cartagena | 262 | 2h 50m |
| Cartagena → Sevilla | 540 | 5h 23m |
| Sevilla → Lagos / Algarve | 276 | 2h 52m |
| Lagos → Montegordo | 135 | 1h 23m |
| Montegordo → Salamanca | 606 | 5h 48m |
| Salamanca → Bilbao | 397 | 4h 00m |
| Bilbao → Hossegor | 177 | 2h 01m |
| Hossegor → Biarritz | 40 | 0h 37m |
| Biarritz → Zurich | 1,167 | 11h 38m *(split 2 days)* |
| **Total** | **~5,208 km** | **~54h driving** |

Plan for **8–10 driving days**. At 8h/day that's ~7 days of pure transit; the rest is stops, camps, and wandering.

---

### Driving Legs & Waypoints

#### 🏔️ Zurich → Ardèche → Provence

- **Gorges de l'Ardèche + Rives d'Arc campground** — Entry into southern France. The gorges are kayakable with young kids on the calm sections; the campground sits right on the river.
- **Aix-les-Bains** — Quieter alpine lake town; good market. Works well as an overnight stop before the Ardèche.

#### 🌿 Provence & Marseille

- **Cassis** — Small fishing port; Calanques entry for families. Walk to Calanque de Port-Miou.
- **Luberon / Lourmarin** — Go via Lourmarin (least touristic of the Luberon villages) and L'Isle-sur-la-Sorgue on a Sunday for the open-air antique market and food stalls.
- **Salin d'Aigues-Mortes** — Pink salt flats; medieval walled town; salt co-op sells directly.
- **Parc Ornithologique de Pont-de-Gau (Camargue)** — Flamingos at eye level, wooden walkways, no hiking required. Right next to Saintes-Maries-de-la-Mer.
- **Marseille** — Keep it short: focus on Le Panier + MuCEM. Station Uvale du Palais for a hidden food stop.

#### 🦩 Montpellier Area

- **Sète** — Genuine fishing port; excellent seafood market. Buy fresh fish here for camp cooking.
- **Plage de l'Espiguette (Le Grau-du-Roi)** — One of the last wild, undeveloped beaches on the French Mediterranean.
- **Gruissan** — Salt lagoon village near Narbonne. Circular layout, old tower, salt marsh. Good food market.
- **Collioure** — Coloured harbour town right at the French-Spanish border. Matisse and Derain painted here. Good last stop before crossing into Spain.

#### 🏖️ Costa Brava

- **Peratallada** — Most intact medieval village in Spain that most people haven't heard of. Carved from stone, no traffic.
- **Begur + Cala Aigua Blava** — Architectural anchor of the Costa Brava; Cuban colonial mansions and cliff coves.
- **El Far de Sant Sebastià** — Lighthouse above the cliffs near Begur. Dramatic views, much less visited than the main Begur coves.
- **Cala Sa Boadella** — Hidden beach closest to the curated campgrounds; less visited, no facilities.
- **Camí de Ronda** — Coastal path stitching the whole Costa Brava together. Best section: Calella de Palafrugell to Llafranc.
- **Garrotxa Volcanic Zone** — Approachable volcanic landscape inland; beech forests over ancient craters, short loops.
- **Via Ferrata Cala del Molí** — Activity near wecamp Santa Cristina.

#### 🌆 Barcelona

- **Mercat de Santa Caterina** — Best market for buying food. Fewer tourists than La Boqueria.
- **Poblenou** — Authentic Barcelona without Gothic Quarter density. Els Pescadors is the local restaurant standout.
- **La Caseta del Migdia** — Open-air bar on the back side of Montjuïc with sunset sea views.
- **Mies van der Rohe Pavilion** — Most important building on the trip for architecture.
- **Nømad Coffee** — Most respected specialty coffee in the city.

#### 🌊 Valencia & Sierra Nevada Area

- **Alcossebre** — Between Valencia and Barcelona; small coves, pine forest to the sea, genuinely uncrowded. Good overnight stretch-break.
- **Cala de San Pedro, Cabo de Gata** — Only reachable on foot from Las Negras (30-min trail). Raw cove, wild, most out-of-time place on the whole trip.

#### 🌿 Portugal / Algarve

- **Costa Vicentina Natural Park + Carrapateira** — No development by law. Carrapateira is the village anchor: one bakery, one surf shop, one restaurant.
  - **Pont'a Pé** — Food stop in Carrapateira.
  - **Moagem in Aljezur** — Coffee stop.
- **Lima Escape Camping & Glamping** — Northern Portugal / Minho. Pair with Cascata do Arado in Peneda-Gerês for a waterfall family hike.

#### 🏜️ Montegordo → Salamanca

This is the longest single leg on the trip at **606km / 5h48m**, and the only one that genuinely needs to be planned as a two-day crossing rather than a single drive. From the eastern Algarve you're heading northeast across the full width of the Iberian Peninsula through some of its least-populated terrain.

**Route trade-off:** Two main lines exist here.

- **Via the west (direct)** — Cross into Spain at Badajoz and drive north through Extremadura to Salamanca. Flat, fast, not particularly scenic. Works if you're short on time and want to reach Salamanca cleanly.
- **Via the east (Albarracín detour)** — Cross into Spain further east, arc up through Andalusia toward Murcia, then cut northeast through the Iberian highlands via Albarracín before descending to Salamanca. Adds distance but the Albarracín stop earns it.

**Waypoint stop:**
- **Albarracín** — Medieval pink-stone village in the Iberian highlands, built into a canyon wall above the Guadalaviar river. It looks assembled rather than constructed — pink and terracotta rock, tight lanes, no straight lines anywhere. Almost entirely car-free within the walls. One of the most architecturally distinctive small towns in Spain and almost nobody outside Spain knows it. If splitting the leg over two nights, this is the obvious overnight.

**Practical note:** Whichever line you take, plan for an overnight somewhere along this crossing. Driving it straight through in one day wastes both the evening and the landscape.

#### 🎣 Basque Country

- **Getaria** — Best txakoli wine village. Grilled fish on open embers directly at the harbour.
- **Mundaka** — Surf village at the mouth of the Urdaibai estuary (UNESCO biosphere). Pairs with Guernica nearby.
- **Guernica (Gernika-Lumo)** — The most historically resonant town on the whole trip. The oak tree, the Basque parliament, the market town — and the weight of what happened here. 15 minutes from Mundaka; the reason to go is the town itself, not just a drive-by.
- **Chillida Leku** — Sculpture park in a forest. Eduardo Chillida's large steel and stone works. Don't skip.
- **Pasai Donibane** — Tiny boat-accessed fishing village 15 minutes from San Sebastián.
- **Hondarribia** — Most beautiful walled town in the Basque Country. Market sells excellent cheese and charcuterie.
- **Lukas Gourmet Shop, San Sebastián** — Best deli in the city; conservas, local txakoli, aged cheeses. Buy provisions for the Landes stretch.

#### 🌲 Landes & Hossegor

- **Capbreton** — Working fishing port next to Hossegor. Buy fish directly at the quay.
- **Lac d'Hossegor** — Tidal lake behind the beach; calm water, pine shade, kayakable. Better for small kids than open Atlantic.

#### 🏔️ Return Leg — Biarritz → Zurich

This is the longest single drive on the route at **1,167km / 11h38m** and must be split over at least two days. Three meaningful route variants exist, each pairing with a different campground for the overnight stop.

**Option 1 — Via Dordogne / Périgord Noir** *(most scenic, slowest)*
> Biarritz → Bayonne → Bordeaux → Sarlat-la-Canéda → Clermont-Ferrand → Lyon → Zurich
- Takes you through the Dordogne valley, one of France's most beautiful inland regions — limestone cliffs, kayak rivers, medieval villages.
- Overnight camp: **Huttopia Sarlat**, 800m from medieval Sarlat on foot.
- Best for: if you want one more genuinely beautiful French stop and don't mind a slower pace.

**Option 2 — Via Auvergne / Chaîne des Puys** *(best landscape, moderate pace)*
> Biarritz → Bayonne → Toulouse → Clermont-Ferrand → Lyon → Zurich
- Passes through the Massif Central with the ancient Puy de Dôme volcanic chain as the backdrop. Strange, quiet, very un-touristy France.
- Overnight camp: **Huttopia Royat**, 10 minutes from Clermont-Ferrand, at the foot of the Chaîne des Puys.
- Best for: a final night in an unusual landscape that feels completely different from the coastal arc of the rest of the trip.

**Option 3 — Via Alsace / Vosges** *(fastest, cleanest approach to Zurich)*
> Biarritz → Bordeaux → Paris (bypass) → Strasbourg → Zurich
- The most direct northern arc back to Switzerland. The Vosges forest is 45km off the motorway.
- Overnight camp: **Huttopia Forêt des Vosges**, the highest-rated Huttopia in France. Deep Vosges fir forest, forest-only spa, Cahutte wood-and-canvas structures.
- Best for: if you want to close the trip with a genuinely immersive forest night before crossing back into Switzerland.

---

## 2. Towns and Regions

### Priority Places

The places ranked as truly unmissable: **Pont-de-Gau bird park**, **Sète market**, **Peratallada**, **Chillida Leku**, **Cala de San Pedro**, and **Capbreton** for fresh fish. These are the ones hardest to replicate anywhere else on the route.

### Worth Elevating in the Shortlist

- **Arles** — One of the strongest Provence towns for your style: market life, food, history, and easy family value. More than a secondary Provence stop; a very good anchor for the whole Camargue / Luberon stretch.
- **Cacela Velha / Praia de Cacela Velha** — One of the most selective Algarve choices in the file, and especially aligned with the quieter, low-development preference. Exactly the kind of place that distinguishes this trip from a more generic Algarve route.

| Area | Place | Why it matters | Label |
